\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\title{Task-Specific Convergence and Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hybrid recurrent architectures that interleave attention layers with linear recurrence are increasingly standard, yet how attention affects per-example behavior across different recurrent backbones remains poorly quantified. We conduct a systematic analysis using the controlled model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}: three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters), evaluated on 10 benchmarks with per-example analysis on LAMBADA ($N = 5{,}153$) and ARC-Easy ($N = 2{,}376$). Aggregate convergence is task-specific: only LAMBADA shows monotonic reduction in cross-architecture variance ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; $\sigma$ drops 79\%; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$), complete at 1.3B but non-monotonic at 340M. Per-example predictive multiplicity, however, is general: on both benchmarks, architectures help and hurt different examples when attention is added (Jaccard $\leq 0.13$, all $p < .01$; ambiguity $\alpha \approx 0.21$--$0.30$ stable across attention ratios). On LAMBADA, aggregate and per-example trajectories are decoupled: $\sigma$ decreases gradually while per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention. On ARC-Easy, per-example divergence persists without any aggregate convergence ($\rho = -0.20$, n.s.). These results establish predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} as a general property of hybrid architectures, independent of aggregate performance equalization.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis has several limitations that constrain the scope of its conclusions.

\paragraph{Statistical power.} With only $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable two-tailed $p$-value under an exact permutation test is approximately 0.008. This ceiling limits our ability to detect weak convergence effects and contributes to the Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ for LAMBADA not reaching conventional significance. We report confidence intervals as a complementary characterization to partially address this constraint.

\paragraph{Per-example scope.} Our per-example analysis covers two benchmarks (LAMBADA, ARC-Easy) spanning retrieval and mixed-reasoning tasks. Extending to additional benchmarks and task types would strengthen the generality claim, particularly for comprehension tasks (e.g., BoolQ) where different per-example patterns may emerge.

\paragraph{Scale coverage.} We compare two scale points (340M and 1.3B parameters). The contrast between complete convergence at 1.3B and partial convergence at 340M is a binary observation, not a scaling law. Intermediate and larger scales would be needed to characterize the functional relationship between model capacity and convergence.

\paragraph{Architecture coverage.} Our study examines three recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) from one model zoo. Other hybrid families, including Jamba, RWKV-6, and Hymba, use different mixing strategies that may produce different per-example patterns. Generalization beyond these three architectures requires additional controlled experiments.

\paragraph{Observational design.} Our attention weight analysis is descriptive, not causal. We report cosine similarities and entropy differences between helped and hurt examples but cannot determine whether attention patterns cause the per-example effects or merely correlate with them. Interventional methods such as attention head ablation would be necessary to establish causal links.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All models in our study are evaluated after pre-training without fine-tuning. Per-example effects may change under task-specific adaptation, and the decoupling pattern we observe may not persist after fine-tuning.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work analyzes publicly available pretrained language models from the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. No new data collection, human subjects research, or model training is involved. We identify per-example behavioral differences across architectures, which may inform practitioners about the limitations of aggregate benchmark comparisons but does not introduce new risks.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Introduction}

When attention layers are added to three different recurrent architectures, their LAMBADA performance becomes near-identical: cross-architecture standard deviation drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. Yet this aggregate convergence hides an asymmetry at the per-example level. The three architectures reach similar overall accuracy by helping and hurting different examples with low overlap (pairwise Jaccard at most 0.11, approximately twice the independence baseline). Two models can agree on a benchmark score while disagreeing on which inputs they get right. This tension between aggregate agreement and per-example disagreement arises in the context of a rapid architectural shift toward hybrid recurrent-attention models, which interleave attention layers within linear recurrent backbones. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba}, Griffin \citep{de2024griffin}, RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle}, and RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} each combine recurrence with attention using different mixing strategies. These systems now serve as production-grade alternatives to pure Transformers, and the practical question of how much attention to include is typically answered by comparing aggregate benchmark numbers. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo spanning three recurrent architectures, five attention ratios, and two parameter scales, which enables systematic study of how attention allocation affects these hybrid designs.

Most evaluations of hybrid architectures compare aggregate benchmark scores and report that a given attention ratio achieves a certain accuracy averaged across tasks or averaged across architectures. This practice can mask substantial per-example behavioral diversity. \citet{marx2020predictive} formalized this risk as predictive multiplicity: a prediction problem admits competing models with conflicting predictions on individual inputs. \citet{damour2022underspecification} documented a related phenomenon, underspecification, showing that models trained from the same pipeline with different random seeds can disagree on individual predictions despite equivalent aggregate performance. \citet{mccoy2020berts} demonstrated a concrete NLP instance: BERT models trained from different random seeds diverge on specific linguistic constructions. These studies examine variation from initialization or training noise within a single architecture. Whether predictive multiplicity extends to hybrid recurrent architectures, where the source of variation is the recurrent backbone itself, remains unquantified. The question matters because practitioners increasingly choose between recurrent backbones based on aggregate benchmarks; if architectures that score identically in aggregate behave differently on individual inputs, such choices carry hidden consequences for deployment reliability and ensemble construction.

We present the first systematic per-example analysis of how attention affects cross-architecture agreement in hybrid recurrent models. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, we compare three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios (0\% to 25\%) and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). These three architectures represent distinct approaches to linear recurrence: gated delta rules, multi-scale exponential decay, and gated state expansion. We evaluate all 30 configurations on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories. On LAMBADA ($N = 5{,}153$) and ARC-Easy ($N = 2{,}376$), we conduct per-example decompositions, classifying each example as helped, hurt, or unchanged when attention is added, and measure cross-architecture overlap using Jaccard similarity. Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling} previews our central result: aggregate convergence and per-example divergence follow different trajectories as attention ratio increases.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_decoupling_hero.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled. (a) Cross-architecture standard deviation on LAMBADA decreases gradually with attention ratio at 1.3B (solid) but partially rebounds at 340M (dashed). (b) Pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped/hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat.}
\label{fig:decoupling}
\end{figure}

Our analysis yields three findings. First, attention-driven convergence is task-specific. Of 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA exhibits monotonic reduction in cross-architecture variance ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops 79\%). LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada} is a pure context-retrieval task that requires predicting the final word of a passage from long-range dependencies; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, which offers a natural explanation for why convergence occurs on this task and not others. Benchmarks that involve reasoning or comprehension show no convergence, with all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$ and $p \geq .233$. The Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict correction at $\alpha = 0.05$; the confidence interval, which excludes zero, provides a complementary characterization of the effect. Second, convergence depends on scale. At 1.3B parameters the $\sigma$ trajectory is monotonically decreasing, but at 340M it is non-monotonic: $\sigma$ partially rebounds at the highest attention ratio, with only 36\% net reduction versus 79\% at 1.3B. This contrast provides a preliminary observation that sufficient model capacity may facilitate complete equalization on retrieval. Third, and central to our title-level claim, per-example predictive multiplicity is a general property of hybrid architectures, independent of aggregate convergence. On LAMBADA, aggregate $\sigma$ decreases gradually ($0.024 \to 0.005$) while pairwise Jaccard of per-example effects saturates at 4\% attention (${\sim}0.095$)---the two quantities are decoupled. On ARC-Easy, no aggregate convergence occurs ($\rho = -0.20$, n.s.), yet per-example overlap is comparably low (Jaccard 0.07--0.13, all $p < .01$) and the ambiguity $\alpha$ of \citet{marx2020predictive} is 0.21. Across both benchmarks, $\alpha$ is stable at 0.21--0.30 regardless of attention ratio.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We quantify task-specific convergence: only retrieval tasks (LAMBADA) show attention-driven cross-architecture convergence at 1.3B scale; reasoning and comprehension benchmarks do not (\S\ref{ssec:task_convergence}, Table~\ref{tab:convergence}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}).
    \item We observe scale-dependent convergence: the monotonic convergence pattern at 1.3B becomes non-monotonic at 340M, a preliminary observation that sufficient scale may facilitate complete equalization (\S\ref{ssec:scale_convergence}).
    \item We demonstrate that per-example predictive multiplicity is a general property of hybrid architectures (ambiguity $\alpha \approx 0.21$--$0.30$ across retrieval and reasoning tasks), independent of whether aggregate performance converges (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}, \S\ref{ssec:decoupling}, Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

We first ask whether adding attention layers reduces performance differences between architectures, and whether any such reduction is uniform across tasks. For each benchmark, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation ($\sigma$) at each attention ratio and measure the Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) between attention ratio and $\sigma$ across the five configurations.

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports results for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ (exact permutation test, $p = .017$, two-tailed, $n = 5$, 120 permutations). Cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. The 95\% BCa percentile bootstrap confidence interval for $\rho$ is $[-1.0, -0.7]$ (10{,}000 resamples), which excludes zero. After Bonferroni correction for six confirmatory benchmarks, the adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict Bonferroni correction at $\alpha = 0.05$. Benjamini--Hochberg false discovery rate control yields a similar adjusted $p$ (0.102), as only one of six benchmarks approaches nominal significance. The confidence interval provides a complementary characterization of the convergence effect, indicating a reliably negative association even though the corrected $p$-value does not reach conventional significance.

LAMBADA is a pure context-retrieval task \citep{paperno2016lambada}; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, consistent with convergence reflecting compensation for differences in how recurrent architectures store contextual information. No other benchmark shows significant convergence (all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$, $p \geq .233$). ARC-Easy and MMLU exhibit moderate negative correlations ($\rho = -0.7$) but non-monotonic trajectories; COPA shows a similar pattern ($\rho = -0.67$) but is limited by $n = 100$ (Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). The decoupling analysis (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) requires a converging benchmark and is demonstrated on LAMBADA; per-example divergence itself extends to ARC-Easy (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks (1.3B scale). $\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ across five configurations. $p$: exact permutation test (two-tailed, 120 permutations). $\sigma$ values reported $\times 10^{2}$ (accuracy percentage points). Ratio: $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$. \textbf{Bold}: only benchmark with $\rho = -1.0$.}
\label{tab:convergence}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
Benchmark & Category & $\rho$ & $p$ & $\sigma_{\text{pure}}$ & $\sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$ & Ratio \\
\midrule
\textbf{LAMBADA} & \textbf{Retrieval} & $\mathbf{-1.0}$ & \textbf{.017} & \textbf{2.41} & \textbf{0.49} & \textbf{4.87} \\
BoolQ & Comprehension & $0.2$ & .783 & 1.63 & 2.36 & 0.69 \\
ARC-C & Reasoning & $0.6$ & .350 & 0.96 & 1.32 & 0.72 \\
HellaSwag & Reasoning & $0.3$ & .683 & 1.17 & 1.18 & 0.99 \\
PIQA & Reasoning & $-0.5$ & .450 & 0.54 & 0.34 & 1.59 \\
WinoGrande & Reasoning & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.67 & 0.78 & 0.86 \\
ARC-E & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 1.00 & 0.13 & 7.70 \\
MMLU & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 0.92 & 0.41 & 2.27 \\
SciQ & Mixed & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.87 & 0.74 & 1.19 \\
XStoryCloze & Mixed & $-0.6$ & .350 & 0.82 & 0.55 & 1.49 \\
\midrule
COPA$^{\dagger}$ & Reasoning & $-0.67$ & .267 & 2.63 & 0.47 & 5.57 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{\dagger}$Supplementary ($n = 100$); excluded from multiple comparison correction.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence}\label{ssec:scale_convergence}

We compare the LAMBADA convergence pattern at two scales. At 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonic: $\sigma$ decreases at every attention level from 0\% through 25\% ($\rho = -1.0$). At 340M, convergence is partial. $\sigma$ reaches a maximum reduction of 56\% at intermediate attention ratios, but rebounds at h3-1, yielding a net endpoint reduction of 36\%: the trajectory is non-monotonic, and the highest-attention model does not achieve the lowest cross-architecture variance. One possible interpretation is that at smaller scale, the capacity overhead of attention layers creates interference that offsets the retrieval benefit.

This contrast is a preliminary observation from two data points (not a scaling law) that scale may facilitate complete equalization. Despite the aggregate divergence, mean pairwise Jaccard similarity is similar at both scales (340M: $J = 0.094$ helped, $0.090$ hurt; 1.3B: $0.100$, $0.093$), extending the decoupling observation (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) to the scale dimension.

\subsection{Per-Example Divergence}\label{ssec:per_example}

Do architectures converge by improving on the same examples? We compare each architecture's pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples, classifying each as helped (correct only in hybrid), hurt (correct only in pure), or unchanged.

Each architecture displays a distinct profile of per-example changes (Table~\ref{tab:per_example}). GDN loses a net 153 examples (462 helped, 615 hurt), RetNet loses 69 (426/495), and HGRN2 gains 153 (548/395). HGRN2 is the only architecture for which attention produces a net per-example benefit. Pairwise Jaccard similarity is low: mean $\bar{J} \leq 0.104$ for helped, $\leq 0.096$ for hurt, indicating ${\sim}90$\% non-overlap. Overlap significantly exceeds the independence baseline ($J_{\text{null}} \approx 0.05$, all $z > 5.9$, $p < .0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:jaccard_permutation}), but normalizing by the theoretical upper bound $J_{\max} = \min(|A|,|B|)/\max(|A|,|B|)$, observed overlap reaches only 5--7\% of the achievable range above chance (normalized ES 0.065 helped, 0.062 hurt; per-pair details in Appendix~\ref{app:normalized_es}), confirming that low Jaccard cannot be attributed to set-size constraints.

No surface-level feature (token count, word frequency, syntactic complexity) reliably separates helped from hurt examples. However, pure-model confidence on the target token is strongly predictive within each architecture: hurt examples have lower target-token probability among pure-correct examples ($d = 0.83$--$0.92$), and helped examples have higher probability among pure-wrong examples ($d = 0.73$--$0.77$); see Appendix~\ref{app:confidence_margin}. A Kruskal-Wallis test confirms cross-architecture structure in both hurt ($p = .0005$) and helped ($p = .020$) distributions. This structured per-example disagreement despite similar aggregate performance constitutes predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} arising from architectural variation rather than random initialization \citep{damour2022underspecification}.

\paragraph{Cross-task generalization.} To test whether per-example divergence depends on aggregate convergence, we repeat the analysis on ARC-Easy ($N = 2{,}376$), a mixed benchmark with no aggregate convergence ($\rho = -0.20$, $p = .783$; $\sigma$ flat at ${\sim}0.10$). Pairwise Jaccard ranges from 0.067 to 0.134 (all 24 pair $\times$ ratio combinations $p < .01$, permutation test). Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity} reports the ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive}---the fraction of examples with cross-architecture disagreement. Ambiguity is substantial on both benchmarks ($\alpha \approx 0.21$--$0.30$) and stable across attention ratios, confirming that per-example multiplicity is not contingent on aggregate convergence.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Predictive multiplicity at 1.3B (h3-1). $\alpha$: ambiguity \citep{marx2020predictive}. $\bar{J}$: mean pairwise Jaccard of helped~(h) and hurt~(u) sets.}
\label{tab:multiplicity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & $N$ & $\alpha$ & $\bar{J}_{h}$ & $\bar{J}_{u}$ & $\rho_\sigma$ \\
\midrule
LAMBADA & 5153 & .30 & .10 & .09 & $-1.0^*$ \\
ARC-Easy & 2376 & .21 & .10 & .11 & $-0.2$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\par\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize $^*\!p = .017$. $\alpha$ stable across ratios (.29--.32 / .20--.23).}
\end{table}

\subsection{Decoupling}\label{ssec:decoupling}

The central finding of this paper is that aggregate convergence and per-example divergence operate on different timescales as attention ratio increases (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).

Cross-architecture $\sigma$ decreases gradually across the five attention ratios: $0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$. Each additional attention layer provides incremental reduction. In contrast, pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects saturates at the first attention level (4\% attention): approximately 0.095 for helped examples, with no further increase at higher ratios. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles increases from 0.013 at 4\% attention to 0.040 at 25\%.

This separation of timescales constitutes a decoupling: attention reduces the aggregate performance gap but does not homogenize per-example behavior. Bootstrap confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples) confirm this statistically: the 95\% CI for $\sigma$ does not overlap between pure $[0.019, 0.030]$ and h3-1 $[0.002, 0.011]$, while the CIs for mean Jaccard overlap completely across all non-zero attention ratios (helped: $[0.08, 0.11]$; hurt: $[0.07, 0.11]$). Within-architecture Jaccard averages ${\sim}0.33$ (Appendix~\ref{app:within_arch_stability}), roughly three times the cross-architecture value, confirming that architecture identity has greater influence than attention dose on which examples are affected.

Attention weight analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:attention_patterns}) provides partial insight: at layer 2, GDN and RetNet produce nearly identical attention distributions (cosine 0.995) yet differ in per-example effects; entropy differences between helped and hurt examples are negligible for GDN and HGRN2 ($|\Delta H| \leq 0.002$), with RetNet showing a small but slightly larger difference ($\Delta H = +0.018$). Per-example effects are not explained by attention weight patterns, consistent with originating in the recurrent layers. On ARC-Easy, where aggregate performance shows no convergence ($\rho = -0.20$, n.s.), per-example ambiguity remains comparable ($\alpha \approx 0.21$ vs.\ 0.30 on LAMBADA; Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}), confirming that per-example architectural disagreement does not depend on aggregate equalization.

\subsection{Robustness}\label{ssec:robustness}

Removing GDN weakens the LAMBADA convergence from $\rho = -1.0$ to $\rho = -0.7$ ($p = .233$), partly confounded with reduced statistical resolution at $n = 2$ architectures. The RetNet--HGRN2 pair preserves the decoupling: all hybrid $\sigma \leq 0.008$ (${\approx}$54\% below pure) while Jaccard saturates at 0.086--0.108, confirming the result is not an artifact of the GDN head-count confound (Appendix~\ref{app:gdn_confound}). Full sensitivity analyses appear in Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}.

